\subsection{What symmetry averaging preserves, and what polar reduction loses}
The source asks for interpolation respecting rank-one convexity and frame indifference. A function $W$ on real matrices is rank-one convex if its restriction to every affine line with rank-one direction is convex. We test two possible routes: average an already valid interpolant over a finite symmetry group, or interpolate directly in polar coordinates.
\begin{proposition}
Let a finite group $\Gamma$ act linearly by maps $T(F)=QFR$, with $Q,R$ invertible. If $W$ is rank-one convex, then $\overline W(F)=|\Gamma|^{-1}\sum_{T\in\Gamma}W(T(F))$ is rank-one convex and invariant under $\Gamma$. If a sample set is closed under $\Gamma$, its specified values are constant on each orbit, and $W$ interpolates them, then so does $\overline W$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $H$ has rank one, $QHR$ also has rank one because multiplication by invertible matrices preserves rank. For any $F,H$ with this property, each summand $t\mapsto W(T(F)+tT(H))$ is convex; their average is convex. For $T_0\in\Gamma$, composition $T\mapsto T\circ T_0$ permutes the group, proving $\overline W(T_0(F))=\overline W(F)$. At a sample point $F$, every $T(F)$ is another sample with the same specified value. Each summand therefore equals that value, proving interpolation.
\end{proof}
This route presupposes existence and construction of $W$; averaging does not produce one from inconsistent or otherwise difficult data. Finite-group invariance also does not by itself imply invariance under all rotations.
For the second route consider the rank-one affine line
\[
F(t)=\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad U(t)=(F(t)^TF(t))^{1/2}.
\]
Its polar stretch values satisfy
\[
U(0)=I,\qquad U(\pm1)=\frac1{\sqrt5}\begin{pmatrix}2&\pm1\\\pm1&3\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Squaring the last matrices gives $F(\pm1)^TF(\pm1)$, and they are positive definite because their leading entry and determinant are positive. Hence they are the positive square roots. But their midpoint is $\operatorname{diag}(2,3)/\sqrt5\ne I$. The polar map therefore does not carry this rank-one line to the corresponding straight line in stretch space.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
The two matrix squares, determinants and midpoint inequality were checked exactly up to the displayed symbolic square root. The attempted inference that ordinary straight-line interpolation of stretch matrices automatically checks rank-one convexity fails at this geometric step. This does not prove that all methods using polar coordinates fail; they could track the resulting curved paths or impose stronger sufficient conditions. No existence theorem or algorithm for a general frame-indifferent rank-one-convex interpolant is obtained, and the report's stated approximation results are not claimed as new.

